Reason:
The URB was not anchored before being submitted. As a result, if `usb_kill_anchored_urbs()` is called (e.g., during device removal), the URB could not be found and killed, potentially causing a memory leak or use-after-free.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
	usb_anchor_urb(urb, &es58x_dev->rx_urbs);
	ret = usb_submit_urb(urb, GFP_ATOMIC);
	if (!ret)
		return;
	usb_unanchor_urb(urb);
Reason:
The URB is anchored before submission, ensuring it is part of the anchored list so that `usb_kill_anchored_urbs()` can properly cancel it. If submission succeeds, the function returns immediately with the anchor intact (the URB remains anchored until the completion callback handles it or it is killed). If submission fails, the URB is unanchored to avoid leaving a dangling reference in the anchored list. This pattern prevents the URB from being leaked or used after it is freed.

